### Example 2 — Entity + single metric across 3+ entities → chart (NOT kpi_row)

**Input brief:**
Title: City-wise ROAS — Q3 FY26
Narrative role: Show ROAS by city to identify where ad spend is profitable and where it is not.
Key messages:
1. Bengaluru ROAS 0.45x
2. Hyderabad ROAS 0.31x
3. Chennai ROAS 0.28x
4. Mumbai ROAS 0.21x
5. Only Bengaluru is above the break-even threshold of 0.40x
Visual emphasis: Bengaluru is the clear leader; the other three cities underperform — bar chart shows the gap at a glance

**Correct output:**
```json
{
  "slide_type": "data",
  "components": [
    {
      "component_id": "slide_title",
      "kind": "title",
      "weight": "minor",
      "content_data_json": {"title": "City-wise ROAS — Q3 FY26"}
    },
    {
      "component_id": "city_chart",
      "kind": "chart",
      "weight": "hero",
      "design_hint": "add a horizontal reference line at 0.40x labeled break-even; use accent color for the Bengaluru bar, muted color for the rest",
      "content_data_json": {
        "chart_type": "bar",
        "chart_title": "ROAS by City",
        "chart_labels": ["Bengaluru", "Hyderabad", "Chennai", "Mumbai"],
        "chart_values": [0.45, 0.31, 0.28, 0.21]
      }
    },
    {
      "component_id": "threshold_note",
      "kind": "narrative",
      "weight": "supporting",
      "content_data_json": {"text": "Only Bengaluru is above the break-even threshold of 0.40x"}
    }
  ],
  "layout_hint": "city_chart is the hero; threshold_note is a slim supporting band below it"
}
```

**Why chart and not kpi_row:** Four cities each with ONE metric is a comparison-across-entities, not a headline-metrics dashboard. A bar chart communicates rank and gap at a glance. kpi_row would render four equal tiles with no visual sense of order or distance.

