### Example 3 — Categorized list → bullet_list (NOT one component per category)

**Input brief:**
Title: Campaign Winners and Losers — Q3 FY26
Narrative role: Summarise which campaign segments are working and which need action.
Key messages:
1. Winners: brand exact, paneer (ROAS > 1.0x)
2. Breakeven: dairy exact, instant food (ROAS 0.40–0.60x)
3. Losing: butter chicken phrase, ready meals broad (ROAS < 0.40x)
4. Immediate action: pause butter chicken phrase and reallocate ₹12L to brand exact
Visual emphasis: The winner/loser split is the headline; action item should stand out

**Correct output:**
```json
{
  "slide_type": "content",
  "components": [
    {
      "component_id": "slide_title",
      "kind": "title",
      "weight": "minor",
      "content_data_json": {"title": "Campaign Winners and Losers — Q3 FY26"}
    },
    {
      "component_id": "segment_list",
      "kind": "bullet_list",
      "weight": "hero",
      "design_hint": "color-code by sentiment — green prefix for Winners, amber for Breakeven, red for Losing; bold the action item",
      "content_data_json": {
        "bullets": [
          "Winners (ROAS > 1.0x): brand exact, paneer",
          "Breakeven (ROAS 0.40–0.60x): dairy exact, instant food",
          "Losing (ROAS < 0.40x): butter chicken phrase, ready meals broad",
          "Action: pause butter chicken phrase and reallocate ₹12L to brand exact"
        ]
      }
    }
  ],
  "layout_hint": "segment_list is the whole slide — centered, generous line height"
}
```

**Why one bullet_list and not three separate components:** Items under different category labels (Winners / Breakeven / Losing) are still a SINGLE list of parallel statements. Splitting into three components would create three separate visual blocks for no reason. bullet_list supports inline formatting to differentiate the categories visually.

